To quantitatively obtain the droplet’s size from imaging data, we developed a custom-made Matlab code64. First, maximum intensity projections (MIP) of the measured z-stack of multiple droplets in a region of interest were obtained for the inner droplet at every timepoint. We focused on the inner droplet because changes in outer droplet volume follow changes in inner droplet volume (Fig. 2e). Individual timelapses of inner droplets’ MIPs were segmented by thresholding a grayscale image with an input threshold value. Segmentation artifacts smaller than a critical object size are removed and binary erosion operation and binary dilation operation are applied consecutively to generate smooth droplet interfaces. Individual droplets were then labeled at each time point and tracked over time based on the shortest distance criterion between consecutive time points. For each droplet identified in the segmented image, the droplet area \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
